- Story & Context: Confined inside the circus menagerie’s wintering greenhouse the morning after the ceremony, Lucinda Delmonico spent dawn grafting wild privet cuttings onto sweet orange saplings while her father’s aerial spotters circled the glass exterior with shotguns. With hands stained purple by elderberry juice and engine grease, she plugged a cracked valve trombone into a high-gain tape machine, laying brittle, syncopated ska chanks over Wayne’s bass track humming through the iron irrigation pipes. When her kin smashed the greenhouse transom to drag her back to the high-wire caravan, she simply opened the throttle on her amplifier, shattering three rows of frosted glass with an unyielding 130 BPM groove while shouting into a rusted dispatch mic: "You can graft the snake to the citrus bough, but it still strikes west."
