- Story & Context: Decades after the circus dynasties burned their wagons for winter firewood, the trampled earth at Black Creek Crossing sprouted an impenetrable ring of ironwood trees that locals claim vibrates at forty hertz whenever the barometric pressure drops. Surviving carny elders still sneak past forestry fences every solstice to set up an off-grid generator, filling the night air with the rich smells of burnt engine oil, wild mint, and rotting sawdust. There, under the hum of humming halogen work-lights, the descendants play the foundational wedding loop until migrating herons drop from the canopy to perch comatose on the speaker stacks. The gathering dissolves only when the crowd chants the epitaph carved into Wayne’s rotted plywood stage: "Nothing dies in the sawdust if you keep the wheel turned."
